<%# locals: (slate:, team_rows:) %>
<%#
    The two lines, drawn. A span with a bye in it prices on TWO lines, and the
    prose above says so in words — this says it in one picture: the same curve,
    twice, the bye line riding 1.5x higher.

    INLINE SVG, no chart library. The admin slate page pulls Chart.js off a CDN,
    but this page is public, uncached and mobile-first, and two staircases do
    not earn 80 KB of JavaScript and a third-party origin. No script runs here.

    WHAT THE MARKS MEAN, and why the distinction is worth having:
      * the LINES are the rule    — SlateMatchup.turf_score_for, per rank
      * the DOTS are the prices   — each team's STORED turf_score
    They coincide on a normally-priced slate. A dot OFF its line is a hand-edited
    multiplier (the admin board can write one), which is exactly the thing worth
    seeing and would be invisible in the table below.

    IDENTITY IS NEVER COLOUR ALONE: each line carries a dash pattern, a legend
    entry, and (where there is room) a direct label at its right end. That is
    also what licenses the light-mode palette — its green sits under 3:1 against
    the card surface, and labels plus the table below are the relief the
    contrast rule asks for.
%>
<% teams = team_rows.size %>
<% lines = benchmark_lines(slate, teams) %>
<% if teams > 1 && lines.first.points.any? %>
  <%# THE FRAME HOLDS THE DOTS, NOT ONLY THE LINES. A stored multiplier is
      hand-written with no bound, so it can sit above the top of its line or
      below the floor, and sized to the lines alone the SVG clipped away exactly
      those marks. Both ends, so an underpriced team is as visible as an
      overpriced one. The helper owns the arithmetic; an ordinary slate gets
      back the frame it always had. %>
  <% y_min, y_max = benchmark_y_domain(lines: lines, team_rows: team_rows) %>
  <% rows_by_factor = team_rows.group_by { |row| row.game_factor > 1 ? "bye" : "full" } %>

  <figure class="card p-4 mb-6" data-testid="benchmarks-chart">
    <figcaption class="text-secondary text-sm mb-3">
      <span class="text-heading font-semibold">Points per game &times; rank.</span>
      Every team is ranked on projected points per game; the line it sits on is set by how many games it plays here.
    </figcaption>

    <%# Legend — always present for two series, and the only place the colours
        are named. The text wears the page's own ink; the swatch carries
        identity, dash and all. %>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 mb-2" data-testid="benchmarks-chart-legend">
      <% lines.each do |line| %>
        <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs text-secondary">
          <svg width="22" height="8" aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">
            <line x1="1" y1="4" x2="21" y2="4" stroke="var(--bench-<%= line.key %>)" stroke-width="2.5"
                  stroke-linecap="round"<%= line.key == "bye" ? ' stroke-dasharray="9 5"'.html_safe : "" %>></line>
          </svg>
          <%= line.label %>
        </span>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <%# TWO RENDERS, ONE PICTURE. A viewBox scales its text along with the box,
        so a single chart cannot be legible at 390px AND at 900px: sized for the
        desktop, its axis type lands near 6px on a phone. The narrow render is
        therefore its own geometry — a tighter box, bigger type, fewer rank
        ticks, and no end labels, since the legend above already names both
        lines. Same helper, same points; only the frame changes. %>
    <div class="sm:hidden">
      <%= render "benchmarks/two_line_svg", lines: lines, rows_by_factor: rows_by_factor,
            teams: teams, y_min: y_min, y_max: y_max, compact: true %>
    </div>
    <div class="hidden sm:block">
      <%= render "benchmarks/two_line_svg", lines: lines, rows_by_factor: rows_by_factor,
            teams: teams, y_min: y_min, y_max: y_max, compact: false %>
    </div>
  </figure>
<% end %>
